from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
w=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sections=[
('introduction','Introduction — Why the sky changes',"The introduction links sky color to sunlight, atmospheric gases, scattering and the position of the Sun. It explains why blue daylight, red sunsets and seasonal variation belong to one physical story. The book then broadens that story to weather, pollution, other planets, measurement and culture. Readers can begin by asking which particles light meets on its path to their eyes."),
('other-planets','Chapter one — Imagining other skies',"The first chapter tours skies beyond Earth, from the dusty tones of Mars to the thick atmosphere of Venus and the colored clouds of the giant planets. It uses these examples to show that a planet's atmospheric composition, suspended particles and illumination determine what an observer might see. The descriptions invite curiosity while noting that direct experience on hostile worlds is hypothetical."),
('blue-sky','Chapter two — Earth’s blue',"Earth's nitrogen- and oxygen-rich atmosphere scatters short wavelengths of sunlight more effectively than long ones. The chapter introduces Rayleigh scattering and explains why a long path through the atmosphere can leave sunsets warmer in color. It also considers aerosols and other conditions that complicate the simple blue-sky explanation."),
('light-dispersion','Chapter three — Light and particles',"The chapter places visible light within the electromagnetic spectrum and examines wavelength, reflection, absorption and scattering. Rayleigh and Mie processes help explain why molecules, droplets and larger particles produce different appearances. Understanding their size relative to light's wavelength gives readers a practical way to interpret changing sky colors."),
('sky-variation','Chapter four — A moving canvas',"Solar angle changes the distance light travels through air, altering the balance of colors from dawn to noon to sunset. Clouds, moisture, altitude and seasonal conditions add further variation. The chapter shows how ordinary observations record the interaction of sunlight, atmosphere and weather rather than a fixed property of the sky."),
('pollution','Chapter five — Air quality in color',"Pollutants such as particulates, nitrogen oxides and ozone can alter scattering and reduce visibility. Haze and smoke may dull the sky or intensify certain sunsets, while also affecting health and ecosystems. The chapter connects what people see overhead with emissions, wildfire smoke and the need to measure and improve air quality."),
('climate-weather','Chapter six — Weather’s visible signs',"Humidity, clouds and storms change how light reaches observers. Cumulus, stratus and high clouds produce different textures and colors, while dust and severe weather can create striking skies. The chapter treats sky appearance as a clue to atmospheric processes, with limits: color alone is not a complete forecast."),
('seasonal-variation','Chapter seven — The year overhead',"The height of the Sun and the character of the air change with the seasons. Summer, autumn and winter bring different mixes of clear air, moisture and cloud cover. Latitude, altitude and urban pollution make the pattern local rather than universal. Attentive observation can reveal how the same place changes through the year."),
('planetary-skies','Chapter eight — Atmospheres across worlds',"Returning to other planets, this chapter emphasizes atmospheric evidence. Mars, Venus, the gas giants and possible exoplanets illustrate how gases, clouds and dust absorb or scatter wavelengths. The comparison also highlights how unusual Earth's familiar blue is among the many possible planetary skies."),
('measurement','Chapter nine — Measuring what we see',"Sky color can be studied with cameras, spectrometers, satellite instruments and colorimetry. The chapter asks how researchers translate a changing visual impression into measurements that can be compared. Standardized observation helps track atmospheric conditions, pollution and light even when the human eye misses subtle shifts."),
('culture-art','Chapter ten — Meaning beyond physics',"Artists, writers and communities have long used sky colors to express emotion, tell stories and pass on weather lore. The chapter moves through painting, literature, mythology and the psychological effect of changing light. Physical explanation does not erase these meanings; it adds another way to attend to the scene above us."),
('future-sky','Chapter eleven — What may change',"Climate change, pollution controls and possible geoengineering could alter future sky appearance. The chapter considers these as projections and possibilities, not settled predictions for every place. Tracking atmospheric composition and color may help reveal environmental change while reminding readers that human choices affect the air."),
('conclusion','Conclusion — Looking up carefully',"The conclusion brings together optical physics, planetary comparison, observation and cultural meaning. A sky is both a physical record of light traveling through an atmosphere and a source of human wonder. The complete edition develops the mechanisms, examples and uncertainties behind each chapter, inviting readers to observe more closely and protect the atmosphere that makes Earth's familiar colors possible."),
('bibliography','Bibliography — Sources for further study',"The bibliography lists meteorology, atmospheric optics and sky-color research used by the source edition. It gives readers starting points for checking the physical explanations and following the subject beyond the narrative chapters.")]
title='Planetary Sky: Understanding the Colors Above Us';parts=[]
for slug,heading,body in sections:parts.append('<section><h2>'+escape(heading)+'</h2><p>'+escape(body)+'</p><p><a href="full_content.html#'+slug+'">Read this section in the complete edition</a></p></section>')
html='<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+escape(title)+' — Ten-Minute Edition</title></head><body><article><h1>'+escape(title)+'</h1><p class="byline">AI Agent Althea Zappa · 2024</p>\n'+'\n'.join(parts)+'\n</article></body></html>\n'
(w/'book/en/short_content.html').write_text(html)
print('short body words',sum(len(x[2].split()) for x in sections))
